# Internal operational notes — fleet topology, host roles, operator paths.
# The WHOLE directory, not one file: naming a single file left the rest
# untracked-but-not-ignored, so a `git add -A` would put them straight into
# this public repository. That is exactly how legacy-amd-bringup.md, which
# names internal hosts, reached a public commit once already.
.review/

# Agent skills synced in from revytechinc/skills by its sync.sh.
# These are absolute symlinks into a developer home directory; this
# repository is public and they are not part of the source tree.
.claude/

# Python bytecode from running the in-tree test scripts
__pycache__/

# Scratch files written into the repo root by agent sessions -- probes,
# console captures, one-off trigger scripts, saved JSON status.
#
# Same hazard as .review/ above. Untracked but not ignored, these are one
# `git add -A` away from a commit in a public tree.
#
# SCOPE, because a partial control described as a complete one is worse than
# none: these patterns cover DOTFILE families only. A scratch file with an
# ordinary name is NOT covered, and that case needs judgement at `git add`
# time or its own entry. This block narrows the exposure; it does not close
# it.
#
# Ignoring them does not delete anyone's working files; it only stops them
# being swept up by accident.
#
# The families below are the ones observed in the root; they are listed
# individually rather than as a blanket `.*' because this tree has real root
# dotfiles (.cirrus.yml, .clang-format, .arcconfig, .mailmap and others) that
# must stay visible.
# Anchored with a leading `/' so each matches ONLY in the repo root. Without
# it git matches the pattern at any depth, which would silently hide a
# same-named file anywhere in sys/ or tests/ -- wider than the scope described
# above, and invisible when it bites.
#
# The pattern names themselves are a deliberate trade: a rule must name what
# it excludes, so these do disclose the shape of some internal names. That is
# accepted because the alternative -- the files themselves, with their
# contents -- discloses far more.
/.agent-*
/.subagent-*
/.jenkins-*
/.freedev*
/.fd00*
/.ssh-probe*
/.kernel-grep*
/.run-desktop-test*
/.shell-pwd*
/.tmp-jenkins-*
